  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Prove that a function $f$ is convex on an interval $I$ if and only if the function $-f$ is concave on $I$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f$ is convex on $I$ if and only if $-f$ is concave on $I$.\n\nMultiplying the defining convexity inequality by $-1$ reverses its direction and gives the concavity inequality for $-f$.",
      "worked": "For all $a,b\\in I$ and $0\\le t\\le1$, convexity of $f$ means\n$$f(ta+(1-t)b)\\le tf(a)+(1-t)f(b).$$\nMultiplying both sides by $-1$ reverses the inequality:\n$$-f(ta+(1-t)b)\\ge t(-f(a))+(1-t)(-f(b)).$$\nThis is exactly the defining inequality for concavity of $-f$. Conversely, multiplying this inequality by $-1$ recovers the convexity inequality for $f$. Hence the two properties are equivalent.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
